Known bugs

The regression tests (tests/run.sh) compare the tools' output with
reference copies in tests/ref, which record what the tools do today,
bugs included.  Each bug below names the reference files that show its
wrong output: fixing the bug changes them, so check the new output and
update the references (tests/run.sh -u) in the same commit, and remove
the entry from this file.

cpp
---

- Token pasting (##) doesn't paste: with "#define CAT(a, b) a##b",
  CAT(var, 1) gives "var 1" and CAT(0x, ff) gives "0x ff", in ANSI
  mode (-A) too.
  References: cpp.i, cpp-A.i, cpp-D.i, cpp-E.i

- #line is ignored: after '#line 500 "renamed.c"', __LINE__ gives the
  real line number (53), and __FILE__ isn't renamed.
  References: cpp.i, cpp-A.i, cpp-D.i, cpp-E.i

- "#if" with no expression isn't an error, and "Cannot open include
  file" doesn't say which file.
  References: cpperr.err, cpperr.i

cc1 (code generation)
---------------------

- Passing or returning unsigned char with ANSI prototypes (the first
  argument and the result in a register) generates 6809 transfers
  between registers of different sizes, which aren't valid
  instructions: "TFR B,X" in the callee, and "TFR PC,D" and "TFR X,B"
  in the caller.
  References: proto.s, proto-O.s, proto.c.cc.err, proto.c.cc.rc

- In PIC mode, global (bss?) pointers and arrays use PC-relative
  addressing rather than direct addressing.  (Not covered by the tests.)

- Taking the address of a direct page object doesn't work: "LEAX <_foo"
  ignores DP.  Some possible sequences instead:
    LDD #_foo ; TFR DP,A ; TFR D,X
    LEAS -1,S ; CLR 0,S ; PSHS DP ; PULS X ; LEAX _foo,X
    TFR D,X ; TFR DP,A ; CLR B ; EXG D,X ; LEAX _foo,X
    TFR D,X ; LDD #_foo ; TFR DP,A ; EXG D,X
  (Not covered by the tests.)

objdump09 (and nm09, size09)
----------------------------

- Object files are rejected with "Bad magic number": objdump86.c checks
  for the x86 dev86 magic number (A3 86), but as09 and ld09 use "S1"
  (OMAGIC 0x5331 in ld/obj.h).  Executables are read.
  objdump01 in retrotinker/mc6801-tools has this fixed.
  References: crt0.o.dump, modes.o.dump, main.o.dump, long.o.dump

ld09 and objcopy09
------------------

- ld09 only writes __btext, __etext and __bdata to the executable if
  the program refers to them, so objcopy09 usually can't find the load
  addresses itself: it warns that the load address is zero and writes
  the program at address 0, unless -T (and -D) are given.
  References: prog-noT.srec, prog-noT.oc.err
